sections += f"""
<section><h2>Appendix A. The three-classroom pilot</h2><p>The pilot dataset was filtered by the three pilot teacher accounts, excluding the demo teacher and its classes. It contains three classroom papers, 118 children, 118 uploaded sheets, and 590 answer records. The three classes were Grade 2A Telangana with 38 children, Grade 4B NCERT with 37 children, and Grade 5A Telangana with 43 children. These counts reconcile exactly: 38 + 37 + 43 = 118; five answers per sheet produce 590 answers.</p><table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Board / subject</th><th>Children</th><th>Topic and method</th><th>Plan time</th><th>Average</th><th>Low / Mid / High</th></tr><tr><td>Grade 2A</td><td>Telangana · Maths</td><td>38</td><td>One-digit addition with carry over · 5-step · Process</td><td>26s</td><td>29.5%</td><td>40% / 26.3% / 25%</td></tr><tr><td>Grade 4B</td><td>NCERT · English</td><td>37</td><td>The Sounds of Bells · 5-step · Process + Product</td><td>31s</td><td>81.1%</td><td>51.1% / 80% / 97.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Grade 5A</td><td>Telangana · Maths</td><td>43</td><td>Everyday measurement problems · 5E · Content + Product</td><td>54s successful</td><td>62.8%</td><td>38.8% / 71.1% / 88.9%</td></tr></table><p>The Grade 2 result is intentionally shown rather than averaged away. Low readiness scoring above Mid and High is an inverted pattern. It may reflect an answer-key problem, assessment difficulty, grouping error, or sheet-reading issue; it is not evidence that the readiness model is correct. The Grade 4 and Grade 5 patterns are more conventional, but still require checking against actual work.</p><p>Planning time was 26–54 seconds for the selected successful plans. Grade 5 had an initial failure after 24 seconds with a misleading API-key message; retries and clearer failure handling allowed the later 54-second run. The recorded full-workflow costs in the coordinator notes were approximately $0.010–$0.021 per classroom. The database token ledger is the authoritative source for the detailed model costs in the Tokenomics section.</p></section>
<section><h2>Appendix B. Pilot feedback and interpretation</h2><p>Four feedback entries were recorded across the three accounts. Ratings were 3, 3, 4, and 5, with a mean of 3.75. The reported Saathi times were two, two, three, and two minutes. Usual planning-time categories ranged from under 15 minutes to over 60 minutes, so the baseline is inconsistent and should not be converted into a precise hours-saved claim.</p><p>Recorded feedback was paraphrased rather than quoted. The Grade 2 feedback requested more rigorous real-life word problems and differentiation inside assignments. The Grade 4 feedback requested story-specific questions and pictures appropriate for Grade 4. The Grade 5 feedback valued differentiation, checking progress, and planning the next class, while also requesting more classroom context. One observation was that a teacher plans four lessons a day and reported completing them in 30 minutes; this is a single recorded observation, not a population estimate.</p><p>The strongest interpretation is operational: the app can produce a structured plan quickly and connect a paper assessment to suggested next actions. The weakest interpretation would be that it improved learning or will save every teacher a fixed amount of time. The pilot cannot support that claim. A proper follow-up would collect pre-specified planning times, observe edits, compare independent baseline/endline assessments, and include comparison classrooms.</p><div class="callout">Privacy choice: no children's names, teacher names, free-text comments, or answer-sheet images are included in the committed evidence export. The redacted CSV preserves feedback ratings, decisions, time categories, and timing fields only.</div></section>
<section><h2>Appendix C. Implementation and measurement detail</h2><p>The interface is intentionally split into a teacher-facing sequence and a code-owned measurement layer. The teacher chooses class context, subject, chapter, topic, lesson type, and optional differentiation. The Lesson Planner produces the lesson vision and method. The Check Builder turns the lesson into an answer key. The teacher prints the check and uploads completed sheets. The Answer Sheet Reader supplies transcription suggestions, while ordinary Python matches roll numbers, applies the answer key, calculates marks, and groups children by readiness. The insight then becomes the input to the Coach and resource builders.</p><p>This division matters because the model should not be trusted to add a child to a group, calculate a percentage, or silently repair an answer key. The code can show exactly which result led to a Low, Mid, or High grouping. Model output is treated as untrusted text: it is validated, reviewed where appropriate, and escaped before rendering. Teacher text is also untrusted and is screened for prompt-injection patterns. Budgets and ownership checks are enforced at the API boundary.</p><p>The app currently confirms uploaded results automatically after reading and marking. That is the accurate behavior in this build. It is not accurate to describe the workflow as waiting for a teacher to confirm a grid. Teacher review before confirmation is therefore a clear next product step, alongside better answer-key checks. The pilot's Grade 2 inversion and Grade 5 initial failure show why that review matters.</p><p>The database also records model calls, cached input, output tokens, provider cost, application cost, and duration. The aggregate evidence uses those rows, filtered to the three pilot teacher IDs. It does not mix demo activity into pilot numbers. A future full-loop trace should join runs, LLM calls, tool calls, review results, and screenshots by run ID; the present report only claims fields that are present in the committed evidence.</p></section>
"""
